akr 0.1
project akr

record akr.observation.a-check-that-reports-nothing/1 : observation {
    title "A check that reports nothing is indistinguishable from a check that found nothing"
    state verified
    scope [ path "crates/**", path "docs/**", path "spec/**" ]
    statement """
        Nine agents audited fifteen AKR workspaces on 2026-09-08 and found the same defect five times,
        in five unrelated subsystems. In each one a mechanism that was supposed to report produced
        silence instead, and in each one the silence was read by every observer as an all-clear.

        ONE. `akr check` did not validate the generated views; only `--views-current` did. Ten of the
        fifteen workspaces were shipping views that did not match their ledger — eight by renderer
        version, one by source-graph hash, one with no views at all — and plain `akr check` reported "no
        diagnostics" on every one. AKR's own repository was among them.

        TWO. AKR-G023 stopped reporting the moment a record became `completed`, and never reported a
        gitignored path at all. A hand census found 93 distinct dead globs across 254 record
        occurrences; 88 of those occurrences sat on completed records and 96 on ignored paths. The
        diagnostic existed, was correct, and was silent on most of what it was built to catch.

        THREE. An acceptance check with `method command` is satisfied by exit status alone, and
        `cargo test <filter>` exits 0 when the filter matches nothing — "running 0 tests ... ok. 0
        passed; 0 failed; N filtered out". Twelve acceptance gates and fourteen evidence commands across
        the workspace named tests that exist nowhere in tracked source; twelve of the evidence records
        carry `result pass`. A git-history census showed every one of the 26 had once named a real test.
        The gate outlived the test, and nothing noticed, because a gate that runs nothing and a gate
        that passes are the same exit code.

        FOUR. A shell pipeline reports its last stage's status, so a recorded command ending in
        `| tail -80` reports success no matter what happened upstream. Three separate agents made this
        error in one afternoon, one of them a step away from sealing a fabricated pass into an evidence
        record.

        FIVE, and the purest form. `Repository::prime_ignored` was handed every scope glob in a ledger
        before any were validated; `git check-ignore -- /abs/path/**` exits 128; `unmatched_watches`
        returned `Err`; and `session.rs` discarded it with `if let Ok(..)`. One malformed glob anywhere
        in a workspace silently switched off every scope and watch diagnostic in that workspace. A
        working AKR-G021 passed its own unit test and could not reach a user.

        The uncomfortable instance is the sixth: this run's own dead-glob census reported zero dead
        globs for three workspaces. Those readings came from the binary, before the fifth defect was
        found. They may have been silences rather than absences, and cannot be trusted until re-run.

        What the five have in common is not a subsystem. It is that each reported an absence of findings
        in a way no reader could distinguish from an absence of checking, and AKR's whole proposition is
        that a reader may trust the ledger without re-deriving it. A tool whose failure mode is
        indistinguishable from success has a specific obligation the ordinary case does not: it must be
        able to say what it looked at, not only what it found.
        """
    observed_at git:0dd6057aafb21ef86ae8d2bc7a659ed3c7fd1ebc
    method manual
    review_after 2027-03-08
}

record akr.observation.concurrent-writes-are-silently-lost/1 : observation {
    title "Simultaneous writers silently lose records, each reporting success"
    state superseded
    scope [ path "crates/akr-cli/src/write.rs", path "crates/akr-core/src/ops/**" ]
    statement """
        The write pipeline has no cross-process concurrency control for a write that adds a new key, so simultaneous writers clobber one another and every one of them reports success. Six `akr papercut` processes started together against one workspace: all six printed `wrote .akr/records/akr/papercuts.akr` and exited 0, and two records landed. The mechanism is the read-modify-write in crates/akr-core/src/ops: `Staged::load` reads every source, the operation edits the ledger in memory, and `Staged::commit` writes each touched file whole through a temporary and a rename. The rename is atomic per file, which is what docs/07-cli.md section 4 promises, but nothing detects that the file changed between the read and the write, so the last writer's copy — built from a snapshot that predates the others — wins. `base_rev` (docs/08-mcp.md) is optimistic concurrency for `revise`, and guards a same-key conflict only; a new key appended to a shared records file passes no such check. Reproduced identically on the unmodified tree at 968ac27 and on the current working tree, so it is not a consequence of the speed work.
        """
    observed_at git:968ac272d86cf36888f22f557aa4ba06d5156f85
    method command
    watches [ "crates/akr-core/src/ops/**", "crates/akr-cli/src/write.rs" ]
}

record akr.observation.concurrent-writes-are-silently-lost/2 : observation {
    title "Simultaneous writers silently lose records, each reporting success"
    state verified
    scope [ path "crates/akr-cli/src/write.rs", path "crates/akr-core/src/ops/**" ]
    statement """
        The write pipeline has no cross-process concurrency control for a write that adds a new key, so simultaneous writers clobber one another and every one of them reports success. Six `akr papercut` processes started together against one workspace: all six printed `wrote .akr/records/akr/papercuts.akr` and exited 0, and two records landed. The mechanism is the read-modify-write in crates/akr-core/src/ops: `Staged::load` reads every source, the operation edits the ledger in memory, and `Staged::commit` writes each touched file whole through a temporary and a rename. The rename is atomic per file, which is what docs/07-cli.md section 4 promises, but nothing detects that the file changed between the read and the write, so the last writer's copy — built from a snapshot that predates the others — wins. `base_rev` (docs/08-mcp.md) is optimistic concurrency for `revise`, and guards a same-key conflict only; a new key appended to a shared records file passes no such check. Reproduced identically on the unmodified tree at 968ac27 and on the current working tree, so it is not a consequence of the speed work.
        """
    observed_at git:968ac272d86cf36888f22f557aa4ba06d5156f85
    method command
    review_after 2027-03-08
    supersedes [ @akr.observation.concurrent-writes-are-silently-lost/1 ]
    verified_by [ @akr.evidence.one-writer-at-a-time-holds-20260908/1 ]
}

record akr.observation.the-normative-layer-is-stuck-at-proposed/1 : observation {
    title "akr.observation.the-normative-layer-is-stuck-at-proposed"
    state verified
    statement """
        Normative records sit at `proposed` in bulk across every ledger in this workspace, and stay
        there. Counted on 2026-09-08: jpegXL-rs with six constraints, seventeen decisions and thirteen
        policies; Lege-ecosystem with thirteen of fourteen live decisions, twelve policies and six
        requirements; LegeOS with eleven of thirty-four live decisions; AKR's own ledger with six of
        twenty.

        The layer is not merely large, it is stuck, and the mechanism is precise. V-021 (AKR-R031)
        refuses to activate a decision that cites no requirement, policy, constraint or evidence. The
        citation an author reaches for first is the observation that motivated the decision — that is
        what `supported_by` most often names — and an observation was exactly the kind V-021 would not
        count, while the diagnostic said only that the decision "cites nothing". Demonstrated in this
        ledger: akr.decision.one-writer-at-a-time had shipped, was re-verified on 2026-09-08, was
        supported_by akr.observation.concurrent-writes-are-silently-lost, and could not be activated
        until an evidence record was written and cited alongside.

        Four Opus agents working four separate project clusters each examined this layer, judged it
        wrong, and each declined to change it — correctly, because clearing it means knowing what every
        decision rests on and guessing is how a ledger acquires fiction. So the cost is not a count of
        proposed records. It is that every future session re-derives the same impasse and writes the
        same paragraph explaining why it did not act.
        """
    observed_at git:0dd6057aafb21ef86ae8d2bc7a659ed3c7fd1ebc
    method manual
    review_after 2027-03-08
}
